WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.230
In just two short minutes, the teaser trailer
for the upcoming "Ahsoka" Disney+ series provides

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:09.670
a wide array of references for "Star Wars"
fans to catch.

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:14.710
The "Ahsoka" trailer hits the ground running,
featuring numerous returning characters from

00:00:14.710 --> 00:00:15.960
"Star Wars Rebels."

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.820
Most ominously, we get our first live-action
look at Grand Admiral Thrawn.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:25.699
Fans have known since Season 2 of "The Mandalorian"
that Ahsoka was after the Chiss military officer,

00:00:25.699 --> 00:00:27.304
but we now know he's coming.

00:00:27.304 --> 00:00:30.585
"Where is your master?

00:00:30.585 --> 00:00:33.096
Where is Grand Admiral Thrawn?"

00:00:33.096 --> 00:00:37.360
To make "Star Wars" fans even more excited,
the titular Jedi mentions that she's heard

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.860
whispers of Thrawn's return "as heir to the
Empire."

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.149
While some may see this as a throwaway line
to simply set up this new villain's presence,

00:00:45.149 --> 00:00:49.440
fans of the extended universe will immediately
recognize the reference to Thrawn's first

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.550
appearance in Timothy Zahn's novel of that
name.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.644
The novel's story takes place five years after
the Battle of Endor and the defeat of the Empire.

00:00:57.644 --> 00:01:02.980
Grand Admiral Thrawn gathers his Imperial
forces, including a clone of Jedi Master Jorus

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.290
C'boath, becoming a massive threat to the
New Republic.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:11.220
The fan-favorite novel is the first in Zahn's
trilogy introducing Thrawn to the "Star Wars"

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.689
universe, and many consider it the go-to Thrawn
story.

00:01:14.689 --> 00:01:19.130
If Lucasfilm is adapting parts of it for the
character's arc in "Ahsoka," then fans are

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:20.549
in for quite the treat.

00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:26.150
Another foreboding figure in the "Ahsoka"
trailer is a darkly dressed Force user played

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:27.700
by Ray Stevenson.

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:32.890
He's not directly named, but he is calm, cool,
and collected, even as he ruthlessly attacks

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:34.369
a group of Rebels.

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:38.399
However, since "Ahsoka" isn't wasting any
time by showing that Grand Admiral Thrawn

00:01:38.399 --> 00:01:42.470
will be a major player, it wouldn't be too
far of a stretch to think that Stevenson's

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.600
character might be from the same book series
where Thrawn originated.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.710
Also introduced in "Heir to the Empire," Joruus
C'baoth is a Dark Jedi that is instrumental

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.110
in many of Thrawn's plans.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:57.990
He is actually a clone of a slain Jedi Master
with nearly the same name, a former friend

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.189
of Emperor Palpatine.

00:01:59.189 --> 00:02:04.540
Clones are usually designated with a misspelled
name in the world of "Star Wars," and considering

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:09.060
that cloning plays a major role throughout
"Star Wars," it would make sense if "Ahsoka"

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.620
features a cloned Jedi to highlight that this
has been a process spanning decades.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:18.129
Whoever Stevenson is playing is sure to be
of great interest, whether it be Joruus or

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:22.360
some other character, and it will be fascinating
to see how he connects to Thrawn.

00:02:23.534 --> 00:02:28.503
"Ahsoka" won't be short of space battles and
threats that will pose challenges for our heroes.

00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:29.879
People will suffer.

00:02:29.879 --> 00:02:31.940
Peril will befall the galaxy.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:36.400
But the people behind the "Star Wars" franchise
also understand that cute furry creatures

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.450
can help launch successful merchandising campaigns
along with serving as comic relief between

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.659
the big battles.

00:02:42.659 --> 00:02:45.810
"Ahsoka" will continue this trend with Tooka
cats.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.390
These orange critters are adorable, but they
do more than sit around looking pretty.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:54.730
Tookas are known for their hunting skills,
and they're mostly used for getting rid of pests.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:58.930
The creatures have appeared in live-action
"Star Wars" products before, most recently

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.220
on "The Mandalorian," where they can be spotted
rummaging through garbage and snarling at Grogu.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.710
The Tooka cat in the "Ahsoka" trailer looks
friendlier than some of its peers, and its

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:12.760
big cameo suggests that it might be the most
significant such feline to grace the "Star

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:14.445
Wars" franchise to date.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:20.430
Near the start of the trailer, Rosario Dawson's
titular character unsheaths her lightsabers

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:24.450
and proceeds to use the Force to spin them
around and open up a hole in the ground.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.709
It seems that she's getting ready for battle,
as she's used the same maneuver during dangerous

00:03:28.709 --> 00:03:30.709
situations in the past.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.330
This move is actually a reference to a big
Ahsoka moment from "Star Wars: The Clone Wars."

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:40.980
She performs a similar move so that she and
Rex can escape a group of clones during Order 66.

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:46.959
It's a subtle moment in the trailer, but eagle-eyed
"Star Wars" fans will instantly recognize it.

00:03:47.308 --> 00:03:52.360
The "Ahsoka" trailer includes a seeming nod
to "Underworld," and we don't mean the unproduced

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.720
"Star Wars: Underworld" TV series, which would
have chronicled organized crime in the city

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:57.720
of Coruscant.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.959
No, the apparent "Underworld" nod here is
to the 2003 film of that name in which vampires

00:04:02.959 --> 00:04:06.510
and werewolf-like Lycans battle for monstrous
supremacy.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:12.190
"For those like me, a death dealer, this signaled
the end of an era."

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:16.850
When Ahsoka cuts the hole in the stone floor
and drops down into an underground lair, it's

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:21.970
definitely reminiscent of when Kate Beckinsale's
vampire hunter, Selene, blasts herself through

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:25.979
a floor and lands the same way in the first
"Underworld" movie.

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:30.700
"Ahsoka" and "Underworld" both revolve around
butt-kicking women, and it's nice to see the

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:34.626
"Star Wars" series give a shout-out to the
entertaining action-horror blockbuster.

00:04:36.039 --> 00:04:40.310
It looks like "Ahsoka" will give "Star Wars
Rebels" fans plenty to chew on as it picks

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.430
up where the animated series left off.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:47.669
At one point in the trailer, Sabine stands
on a tower, watching as Ahsoka's T-6 shuttle

00:04:47.669 --> 00:04:48.669
flies overhead.

00:04:48.669 --> 00:04:51.610
The scene is a direct reference to "Rebels."

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:55.770
In the final episode of that series, the Ghost
crew have all gone their separate ways five

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:57.990
years after the Battle of Endor.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:02.530
Sabine remains on Lothal, watching over the
Capital City from her tower, when Ahsoka's

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:03.789
ship flies past.

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:08.270
The Mandalorian then leaves Lothal with the
Jedi, assisting her in the search for Ezra.

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:13.150
The version in the "Ahsoka" trailer is nearly
a shot-perfect remake, bringing the two characters'

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:14.710
reunion to live action.

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:20.259
"Rogue One" gave fans one of the greatest
"Star Wars" scenes of all time, with Darth

00:05:20.259 --> 00:05:23.260
Vader cutting down an army of soldiers in
a hallway.

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.750
It looks like those New Republic recruits
will be victims of a similar attack in "Ahsoka."

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:32.190
In the trailer, Ray Stevenson's new Sith takes
on a small group of New Republic troopers

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.190
on a ship.

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:37.380
He effortlessly deflects their blaster shots
as he walks closer and closer.

00:05:37.380 --> 00:05:41.660
The brief scene is oddly reminiscent of Vader's
climactic ending in "Rogue One" — not to

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:44.660
mention Darth Maul's hallway scene from "Clone
Wars."

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:48.440
One soldier gets the Force Choke treatment,
while the others don't really look to have

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:49.600
anywhere to hide.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:53.910
"Star Wars Rebels" star Freddie Prinze Jr.
claimed on the "Lights, Camera, Barstool"

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:59.900
podcast that Dave Filoni , the showrunner
of "Ahsoka, directed the iconic "Rogue One" scene.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.480
Early on in the "Ahsoka" teaser trailer, we're
introduced to the live-action version of Sabine

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.130
Wren played by Natasha Liu Bordizzo.

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:11.520
The character first appeared in the animated
series "Star Wars Rebels," which takes place

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.819
in the early years of the Rebellion prior
to the events of the original "Star Wars,"

00:06:15.819 --> 00:06:19.770
Sabine is a Mandalorian warrior who helps
reunite her people by claiming the Darksaber

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:20.770
from her adversaries.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:24.890
Though it's been years since she wielded the
blade, Sabine has no doubt retained all the

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.130
skillsets of a seasoned warrior.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.979
Prior to the ending of "Rebels," Sabine develops
a strong bond with her fellow member of the

00:06:30.979 --> 00:06:35.949
Ghost crew — aka the Spectres — Ezra Bridger,
who disappears suddenly at the end of the

00:06:35.949 --> 00:06:36.949
"Rebels" story.

00:06:36.949 --> 00:06:41.759
In the series finale "Family Reunion – and
Farewell," the Spectres dissolve, with Ahsoka

00:06:41.759 --> 00:06:45.220
and Sabine departing to the stars in search
of their missing ally.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:49.880
Since "Ahsoka" takes place years after "Return
of the Jedi," in-step with "The Mandalorian,"

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:53.380
it's unclear if these two are still hoping
to find their missing comrade or if they've

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.300
given up the search entirely.

00:06:55.300 --> 00:06:59.670
With Thrawn returning to threaten the galaxy,
Sabine will likely be close behind.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:03.880
Since the Grand Admiral was the last one to
see Ezra alive, she'll probably want a few

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:06.830
words with the Imperial warlord regarding
her friend's whereabouts.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:11.690
Nevertheless, as the Mandalorian culture continues
to rebuild, we hope to see Sabine take part

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:13.780
in her people's continued legacy.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.111
Maybe she'll even reunite with Bo-Katan down
the line...

00:07:17.385 --> 00:07:21.629
A longtime staple of the "Star Wars" universe,
Mon Mothma has been a leader in the Rebellion

00:07:21.629 --> 00:07:23.110
for as long as they've been around.

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:28.135
Though she first appeared in "Return of the
Jedi," she was a Rebel long before the original trilogy.

00:07:28.135 --> 00:07:32.560
In fact, actress Genevieve O'Reilly — who
replaced the original trilogy actress Caroline

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.069
Blakiston — first played the character in
George Lucas' third prequel installment "Revenge

00:07:37.069 --> 00:07:39.960
of the Sith," though only in a deleted scene.

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:44.330
O'Reilly returned to "Star Wars" in the standalone
prequel film "Rogue One," and has since played

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.479
a recurring part in the franchise.

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:51.080
After a larger role in the series "Andor,"
O'Reilly returns as the Rebel leader here,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.855
possibly to aid Ahsoka in her search for Ezra
Bridger.

00:07:54.389 --> 00:07:57.960
Though it only appears a handful of times
in the series, the Lothal Jedi Temple was

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.479
a mystic and megalithic site where Jedi Masters
would bring their Padawans to determine their

00:08:02.479 --> 00:08:04.780
promise as potential Jedi Knights.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:09.729
In "Rebels," Ahsoka and Kanan Jarrus take
Ezra to the temple as a rite of passage.

00:08:09.729 --> 00:08:13.780
Eventually, the site — which serves as a
gateway to the World Between Worlds — is

00:08:13.780 --> 00:08:17.560
severed, and access to the Temple is cut off
as it sinks below the surface.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:22.900
Years later, Luke Skywalker attempts to find
the temple in Marvel's "Star Wars" comic series,

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:24.430
only to lose the connection.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.191
Admittedly, Ahsoka might not be at the Lothal
Temple at all, but if she were to attempt

00:08:29.191 --> 00:08:32.710
a connection with the World Between Worlds,
it seems like the first — and possibly only

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.289
— place to start.

00:08:34.289 --> 00:08:39.419
After the Empire takes power, Jedi temples
become few and far between, with many of them

00:08:39.419 --> 00:08:41.250
broken beyond repair.

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:45.510
This is why Luke is seen constructing a brand-new
Jedi temple during the events of "The Book

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:46.510
of Boba Fett."

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:50.680
Whatever Ahsoka's reasons are for investigating
the Temple, it's clear that she has a mission

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.320
that she's going to see through.

00:08:52.969 --> 00:08:56.910
There are plenty of notable astromech droids
that have graced different episodes of the

00:08:56.910 --> 00:09:01.890
greater "Star Wars" canon, and while none
are more famous than the original R2-D2, Chopper

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.450
is in a category of his own.

00:09:03.450 --> 00:09:07.779
Mischievous and a bit prejudiced against other
droids, Chopper isn't so different from your

00:09:07.779 --> 00:09:12.839
standard R2 unit, if you discount the mini
pincers sprouting out of his head.

00:09:12.839 --> 00:09:15.920
Naturally, he sticks out like a sore thumb
in the "Ahsoka" teaser.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.450
Aside from his animated appearances, Chopper
has only shown up in live-action "Star Wars"

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:23.910
on one brief occasion in the form of a cameo
in "Rogue One."

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:28.200
That said, "Ahsoka" will mark the fan-favorite
droid's first official appearance in live-action

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.200
"Star Wars," confirming his place in the post-"Return
of the Jedi" world.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:36.250
And where Chopper is, the rest of the Ghost
crew won't be far behind.

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:40.851
After Garazeb Orrelios made his live-action
debut in Season 3 of "The Mandalorian," it

00:09:40.851 --> 00:09:43.769
doesn't come as a surprise that the rest of
the Spectres would follow.

00:09:44.293 --> 00:09:49.110
Speaking of the Spectres, Hera Syndulla, played
by Mary Elizabeth Winstead, is also seen in

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.560
the "Ahsoka" teaser.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:54.450
Now a general in the New Republic, this Twi'lek
{TWY-lek} pilot has continued the fight long

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:58.350
after the death of her partner Kanan Jarrus
{KAY-nan JAIR-us}, who gave his life to save

00:09:58.350 --> 00:10:00.790
the Ghost crew near the end of "Rebels."

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:04.500
Appearing in "The Clone Wars" spin-off "Star
Wars: The Bad Batch," a young Hera appears

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:09.260
in a two-part episode that provides some necessary
backstory for her hatred of the Empire.

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:10.570
"The Empire's taken my parents.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:13.078
And they are after me now, too."

00:10:13.078 --> 00:10:17.519
Continuing on to "Rebels," Hera works alongside
Ahsoka in a Rebel cell stationed around the

00:10:17.519 --> 00:10:21.829
Lothal system, pushing back against imperial
enemies like Grand Admiral Thrawn.

00:10:21.829 --> 00:10:26.690
Needless to say, Hera has some real experience
and is often considered the best pilot in

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:30.269
the Rebellion — even better than Luke Skywalker
or Han Solo.

00:10:30.269 --> 00:10:34.680
Though her ship, the Ghost, is seen briefly
in "The Rise of Skywalker," it's unclear how

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.660
long Hera outlives her companions in the greater
"Star Wars" timeline.

00:10:38.660 --> 00:10:41.630
Hopefully, "Ahsoka" will keep her in the game
for as long as possible.

00:10:42.079 --> 00:10:46.600
Following her guest appearance on "The Mandalorian,"
Ahsoka was on a mission to find Grand Admiral

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.110
Thrawn and end his reign of terror for good.

00:10:49.110 --> 00:10:54.119
In her way stood Morgan Elsbeth, a servant
of Thrawn's who's completely devoted to the Empire.

00:10:54.119 --> 00:10:58.350
But with the Empire in shambles and the New
Republic on the rise, it stands to reason

00:10:58.350 --> 00:11:02.724
that Elsbeth returned to the shadows to do
her master's bidding outside of the public eye.

00:11:02.724 --> 00:11:07.730
Ahsoka may be tracking her down for more information,
but there's another possibility as well.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:12.010
The droid guards that Ahsoka battles in the
teaser at, presumably, the Jedi temple ruins

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.640
are the same ones who work for Elsbeth.

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:18.130
In fact, Ahsoka and Din Djarin battled them
when their paths first crossed.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:22.269
While we know very little about Elsbeth's
history, many believe that she may actually

00:11:22.269 --> 00:11:26.040
be one of the Nightsisters — a group of
witches from Dathomir.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:30.519
Nevertheless, Elsbeth's return signals that
Thrawn himself isn't far behind and that he

00:11:30.519 --> 00:11:32.221
means serious business.

00:11:32.664 --> 00:11:37.139
Arguably the main character of "Star Wars
Rebels," Ezra Bridger is one of the most impressive

00:11:37.139 --> 00:11:39.540
Jedi to ever travel the galaxy.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:44.520
Trained by his master Kanan Jarrus, Ezra goes
from a Force-sensitive misfit into a full-fledged

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:46.470
Jedi Knight throughout the series.

00:11:46.470 --> 00:11:50.050
After his disappearance at the end of "Rebels,"
what happens to Ezra is one of the biggest

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.280
unanswered questions in all of "Star Wars."

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:56.500
With the deep-space whale-like creatures the
purrgil appearing, albeit briefly, in recent

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.180
episodes of "The Mandalorian," there's hope
that we'll discover where they took Ezra and

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.610
Thrawn after the events of the animated series.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:06.870
With Thrawn's return upon us and plenty of
other animated characters coming to life in

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:11.300
live action, there's little doubt that we'll
be seeing Ezra Bridger soon enough, hopefully

00:12:11.300 --> 00:12:13.120
reunited with his friends.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:17.830
Of course, it's entirely possible that Ezra
— played by Eman Esfandi in "Ahsoka" — won't

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:21.660
appear in anything more than holograms and
flashback sequences.

00:12:21.660 --> 00:12:26.389
But with Thrawn's arrival, it would be a shame
if Ezra didn't make a real appearance as well.

00:12:27.389 --> 00:12:31.079
Only appearing in a single shot in the "Ahsoka"
trailer is the professor droid Huyang 

00:12:31.079 --> 00:12:33.649
, known for his time in service
to the Jedi Order.

00:12:33.649 --> 00:12:37.810
For over a thousand generations, Huyang taught
different Jedi younglings the ways of the

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.170
Force and their Jedi history.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:44.410
Alongside his tutelage, he also instructed
young Jedi on constructing their very own

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:47.399
lightsabers, preparing them for their intensive
Padawan training.

00:12:47.399 --> 00:12:52.090
In "Star Wars: The Clone Wars," Ahsoka travels
with the droid and a group of younglings as

00:12:52.090 --> 00:12:56.300
they gather their kyber crystals, which almost
destroys the ancient professor.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:13:00.540
We don't know what happened to Huyang between
the events of Order 66 and the destruction

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:04.570
of the Empire, but we do know that he seems
to be talking with Ahsoka here.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:08.639
It's possible that this scene is actually
set during the Clone Wars, but it's also possible

00:13:08.639 --> 00:13:13.015
that the old droid survived to the present
day to help rebuild the Jedi Order.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:17.329
While it's admittedly unclear if this is indeed
the famed World Between Worlds seen in "Star

00:13:17.329 --> 00:13:21.440
Wars Rebels," the teaser trailer shows Ahsoka
arriving at a ruined temple.

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:24.750
From the glyphs and maps seen during this
sequence, some have noted that the former

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:27.800
Jedi may be attempting to enter The World
Between Worlds.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:32.550
Later in the trailer, Ahsoka is seen battling
Baylan Skoll in a space that looks somewhat

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.610
similar to Ezra's time in the otherworldly
space.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:40.360
The World Between Worlds from "Star Wars Rebels"
allows its guests to experience and even interact

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:42.100
with various timelines.

00:13:42.100 --> 00:13:46.320
Only Ezra Bridger has been known to break
into this Force-sensitive space, for the sake

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.089
of saving Ahsoka from being killed by her
former master Darth Vader.

00:13:50.089 --> 00:13:54.070
Though he attempted to save his own master
as well, Ezra recognized that the timeline

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:57.759
changes would be too severe and cost the lives
of his friends.

00:13:57.759 --> 00:14:04.250
"I'm sorry, Ezra, but you must see, Kanan
found the moment when he was needed most.

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:05.250
And he did what he had to do…for everyone."

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:08.529
Though we don't know why Ahsoka might be trying
to enter into the World Between Worlds, we

00:14:08.529 --> 00:14:13.194
do know that it's a truly unique space that
the "Star Wars" universe has kept at arm's length.

00:14:13.194 --> 00:14:15.410
Time travel can be dangerous, after all.

00:14:15.959 --> 00:14:20.399
In the "Ahsoka" teaser trailer, we see a young
woman named Shin Hati who wields an orange

00:14:20.399 --> 00:14:23.500
lightsaber and seems to exude dark side energy.

00:14:23.500 --> 00:14:28.499
An apparent villain, Shin is on the hunt for
Ahsoka and might be the apprentice of Baylan Skoll.

00:14:28.499 --> 00:14:33.529
Clad in a dark hood and a darker scowl, her
violent and impulsive nature is a direct contrast

00:14:33.529 --> 00:14:34.940
to that of Ahsoka.

00:14:34.940 --> 00:14:39.050
After years of training under Anakin Skywalker,
Ahsoka learned that her emotions could be

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:42.190
controlled and channelled into something more
useful.

00:14:42.190 --> 00:14:45.980
One addition to Jedi lore that was featured
in the prequel trilogy was the single braid

00:14:45.980 --> 00:14:50.339
that Jedi Padawans wore to signify their tutelage
under a Jedi Master.

00:14:50.339 --> 00:14:53.940
They weren't Masters themselves, and they
were yet to be Knights, making their training

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:56.050
paramount to their education in the Force.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:59.519
In the teaser, Shin seems to sport her own
Padawan braid.

00:14:59.519 --> 00:15:03.570
Though this tradition dies out after the fall
of the Jedi Order, it could make a comeback

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.579
during the New Republic era.

00:15:05.579 --> 00:15:09.779
If Shin and Baylan really are Sith, they may
have ties to our hero's past or "The Clone

00:15:09.779 --> 00:15:10.779
Wars" era.

00:15:10.779 --> 00:15:14.399
On the other hand, the orange lightsabers,
as opposed to the classic red sabers wielded

00:15:14.399 --> 00:15:18.850
by Sith Inquisitors, and padawan braid might
mean that Shin and her master aren't quite

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:20.480
as malevolent as they seem.

00:15:20.804 --> 00:15:24.890
Aside from being a valiant warrior, Sabine
Wren is also an artist.

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:28.440
In the "Ahsoka" teaser, there are a few pieces
of art on her wall while she glances at a

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:31.550
hologram of her fallen companion Ezra Bridger.

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:35.550
Behind her, we see a sketch of a pile of rocks
somewhat reminiscent of the rocks Yoda uses

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:40.630
to train Luke Skywalker's skill, patience,
and concentration in "Empire Strikes Back."

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:43.720
But that isn't the only art worth noting.

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:47.310
Directly to the left of Sabine's head is an
image of a wolf-like creature.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:52.180
This is most likely a Loth-wolf, a species
native to Ezra's homeworld of Lothal that

00:15:52.180 --> 00:15:55.610
often acts as "spirit guides" to Force-sensitive
beings.

00:15:55.610 --> 00:15:59.440
Usually guardians of the light side, the Loth-wolves
aided Ezra in the past.

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:04.459
Likewise, Sabine has drawn a few Loth-cats
on her walls, no doubt companions to the actual

00:16:04.459 --> 00:16:06.279
Loth-cat she owns in her home.

00:16:06.279 --> 00:16:11.449
These felines are also native to Ezra's homeworld
and are likely constant reminders to Sabine

00:16:11.449 --> 00:16:12.449
of his loss.

00:16:13.048 --> 00:16:17.320
The teaser also shows glimpses of a battle
between Ahsoka and a red lightsaber-wielding

00:16:17.320 --> 00:16:20.399
opponent who may be a member of the Inquisitors.

00:16:20.399 --> 00:16:24.300
While most of these assassins were defeated
prior to "Return of the Jedi," it's certainly

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:28.620
possible that there are a few still out there,
hoping to eradicate the surviving Jedi from

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:29.645
the galaxy.

00:16:29.645 --> 00:16:33.800
Throughout "Star Wars Rebels," "Obi-Wan Kenobi,"
and the Marvel Comics, the Inquisitors have

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:38.889
mercilessly hunted nearly every surviving
Jedi post-Order 66, though they failed to

00:16:38.889 --> 00:16:40.670
kill some of the most important.

00:16:40.670 --> 00:16:45.600
Thankfully, Ahsoka seems to gain the upper
hand and proves herself a formidable opponent.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:49.949
After seeing the Inquisitors in live-action
during the Disney+ "Obi-Wan Kenobi" series,

00:16:49.949 --> 00:16:53.810
it would certainly be exciting to see these
villains show up again going forward.

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:57.560
What's especially compelling about this group
of Jedi killers is that many of them were

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:02.690
trained directly by Darth Vader himself, meaning
that much of Ahsoka's training under Anakin

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:05.290
Skywalker most likely overlaps with their
own.

